
<p>
    <strong>Blazor Atoms</strong> is a family of small, self-contained Blazor component
    libraries &mdash; usable in <strong>Server</strong>, <strong>WebAssembly</strong>, or
    <strong>Auto</strong> render mode &mdash; each shipped as its own standalone NuGet package
    with ~zero third-party dependencies. Each library is one "atom": add just the one you need
    and it works.
</p>

<h2>Goals</h2>
<p>
    <strong>Lightweight, drop-in, no lock-in.</strong> Take one component, one library, or
    several, and never inherit a big framework just to use a spinner or a badge.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>No umbrella package</strong> &mdash; no shared runtime dependency tying the libraries together.</li>
    <li><strong>Requires little or no setup</strong> &mdash; no global theme provider, only when necessary <code>builder.Services.Add…()</code> registration, no <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag to wire up by hand (auto import of JS).</li>
    <li><strong>Any render mode</strong> &mdash; the same markup works unchanged in Server, WebAssembly, and Auto unless otherwisse noted in the component library.</li>
    <li><strong>Plays nice</strong> &mdash; intermixes freely with any application, greenfield or legacy, and alongside heavier component suites (MudBlazor, Radzen<sup>&reg;</sup>, Fluent UI<sup>&reg;</sup>, Telerik<sup>&reg;</sup>) without conflict.<sup>*</sup></li>
</ul>
<p><small><sup>*</sup> MudBlazor, Radzen, Fluent UI, and Telerik may be/are trademarks of their respective owners. Mentioned only for compatibility context; no affiliation or endorsement is implied.</small></p>

<h2>Design Philosophy</h2>
<p>
    The goals above are the <strong>ideal</strong> every library is measured against, not a hard
    rule enforced at the expense of the component actually working. Most libraries hit the ideal
    fully &mdash; plain Blazor plus inline SVG and CSS, styled via CSS custom properties, no JS, no
    DI. Where a component's job genuinely can't be done that way, the library breaks the pattern
    on purpose, in the smallest way that satisfies the requirement, and says so in its own README.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Framework-only by default</strong> &mdash; most libraries need nothing beyond
        <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web</code>. A library only reaches further when its
        actual job requires it &mdash; e.g. a barcode/QR encoder is self-contained C#, not a
        third-party codec, by design, not because the rule demands it.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>JavaScript, when a browser primitive truly requires it</strong> &mdash; most
        components are 100% JS-free. Some genuinely can't be: smart tooltip/overlay positioning,
        clipboard access, resize/intersection observers, cursor-follow effects, a 60fps
        <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> drawing surface. In those cases the library ships its own small
        JS module and self-imports it on first use &mdash; still no <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag
        to add by hand, but real JS is running.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>DI/service registration, only where state must be shared</strong> &mdash; most
        components take everything through <code>[Parameter]</code>s, no registration needed. A
        library that coordinates cross-page state (e.g. a breadcrumb trail that needs a page to
        push a resolved title once it loads) cascades a small service instead of forcing global
        <code>services.Add…()</code> wiring &mdash; less setup than a typical DI dependency, but not
        zero.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Raster only when vector genuinely can't do the job</strong> &mdash; charts, gauges,
        sparklines, rating icons, QR/barcodes, and skeleton shapes are all inline SVG. The one
        deliberate exception is <code>BlazorAtoms.Canvas</code>, which owns real
        <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> because real-time freehand ink and PNG export are things SVG
        can't do at acceptable performance.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Culture-safe, trimming/AOT-safe, everywhere</strong> &mdash; invariant number
        formatting in generated markup, and every library, JS-free or not, is verified to work
        under WASM trimming and AOT.
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    <small>
        Bottom line: the philosophy is a strong default, not a straitjacket. Check the library's
        own README for the specifics of what it needs and why &mdash; a component that needs JS or
        DI still keeps everything else about the philosophy (no umbrella package, no forced global
        setup beyond what its job requires, drop-in usage).
    </small>
</p>

